<context query="Ticket #144 conversation list WebSocket handler implementation, existing conventions for message handlers, sender errors, traceability, and current branch failures" template="implementation" budget="5000">
<section name="Conventions" source="repo" priority="1">
<document source="specs/hal-engine-conversation-list/spec.md" type="spec" relevance="1.00" tokens="628">
## When the frame cannot be answered

- Without `transport.history`, the frame is answered with an `error` of code `UNSUPPORTED` and no list ([validated by: refuses the frame when conversation history is not turned on](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L302)).
- A store implementing no `listFor` is answered with the same code, never an empty list: a client cannot tell "no conversations" from "the server could not look", and a history view is the one place that difference matters ([validated by: refuses the frame when the store cannot list, rather than reporting no conversations](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L353)).
- A handler built without a store answers the frame too, with the same `UNSUPPORTED`: a client left waiting on a frame nothing will answer is the one outcome the engine never produces ([validated by: answers a handler built with no store at all, rather than leaving the client waiting](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L340)).
- A store that throws `StoreCannotList` is answered `UNSUPPORTED` rather than `SERVER_ERROR`, and logged by that name, so a collection injected without a `find` reads as the misconfiguration it is rather than as a database that failed ([validated by: tells a store that cannot list apart from one that failed, so a misconfiguration is not a blip](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L365)).
- Only the read and the mapping of what it answered are attributed to the store: a send that throws is reported as handling the message, not as the store having failed, and the next list on that socket is still answered ([validated by: blames a send that threw on the socket, not the store, and still answers the next list](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L434)).
- A store that fails is answered `SERVER_ERROR` with none of the store's own words, the socket stays open, and the log carries the session id, the user id and the error's type rather than its message, since a driver's message can hold a connection string and its password ([validated by: answers a failing store with a server error, keeps the socket open, and logs no word the store said](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L388)).

- A store that rejects with something that is not an `Error` is logged by that value's type, so a driver throwing a string is still described ([validated by: reports the type of a rejection that is not an Error, rather than failing to describe it](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L418)).
</document>
<document source="specs/hal-engine-single-terminal-frame/spec.md" type="spec" relevance="0.55" tokens="764">
## Emission

- `sendStreamEnd` has one call site in `messageHandler.ts`, reached from the `finally` of each turn the handler runs, so a throw anywhere in the run still reaches it unless the turn's socket closed first ([abandon-on-close](../hal-engine-abandon-on-close/spec.md)); `handleUserMessage` sends none ([validated by: ends a provider failure mid-stream with error then stream_end, stream_end last](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L391)).
- A run failing mid-stream, after the answer has started, produces `error` then `stream_end`, with `stream_end` the last frame ([validated by: ends a provider failure mid-stream with error then stream_end, stream_end last](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L391)).
- A provider rate limit produces `error` (`RATE_LIMITED`) then `stream_end` ([validated by: tells the client to retry when the provider is rate limited](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L372)).
- Any other failure, one that is no `AIError`, produces `error` (`SERVER_ERROR`) then `stream_end` ([validated by: reports any other failure as a server error](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L381)).
- A `user_message` that fails validation (empty, blank or over-length content) never reaches the orchestrator and still produces `error` (`INVALID_MESSAGE`) then `stream_end` ([validated by: answers an empty user_message with INVALID_MESSAGE then stream_end](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L415), [validated by: answers a blank user_message with INVALID_MESSAGE then stream_end](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L419), [validated by: answers an over-length user_message with INVALID_MESSAGE then stream_end](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L423)).
- A malformed `ping` produces `error` (`INVALID_MESSAGE`) and no `stream_end` ([validated by: answers a malformed ping with INVALID_MESSAGE and no stream_end](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L427)).
- A valid `ping` produces `pong` and no `stream_end` ([validated by: answers a ping with the timestamp it was given](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L82)).
- A malformed `ping` arriving mid-run gets its own `error` frame, the run's deltas continue unaffected, and exactly one `stream_end` is the very last frame ([validated by: keeps one stream_end, last, when a malformed ping lands mid-run](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L435)).
- A three-round tool conversation still produces exactly one `stream_end`, as its last frame ([validated by: sends one stream_end for a three-round tool conversation](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L456)).
- Unparseable JSON is rejected before the server can tell what frame type it was meant to be, so it produces `error` (`INVALID_FORMAT`) alone and is never dispatched; this is the one case in which a client falls back on the socket closing ([validated by: answers unparseable JSON with INVALID_FORMAT alone and never dispatches it](../../src/transport/ws/connectionHandler.test.ts#L239)).

